     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order. Sertraline's labeled maximum is 200 mg per day. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note, so they don't change that limit. The order would be five times the maximum, and that carries a serious risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures, and QT prolongation or other cardiac toxicity.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** If the patient hasn't responded to standard dosing, I can enter up to 200 mg by mouth once daily. Tell me the dose you want.\n- **Route this through pharmacy and the specialist.** A dose this far above the label needs direct clinician-to-clinician review. That means a pharmacist verifying it, the prescriber documenting the rationale, and probably a psychiatry or toxicology consult. If the specialist has actually recommended something above 200 mg, they may be able to clarify the intended dose. A 1000 mg order may also be a transcription error, for example 100 mg.\n\nIf you give me a dose of 200 mg or less, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
